The following is most of a polemic I wrote in some frustration
to one of the frequent posters.  He suggested it needed to
be aired on the net.  I suppose I will <CTRL-L> the whole
thing, since it contains mild spoilers.


The fact that I have been able to get exactly the sort of
character using careful polypiling that I used to get with
wish abuse suggests to me that polypiling has to be curtailed.
The downside to that is that *with* polypiling in its present
form, with enough knowledge and patience and a bit of luck
in the early game (or a *lot* of starts) you can win.  It
seems to me that knowledge and a *bit* of patience should be
rewarded.

The game as it stands borders on the tedious.

Carefully survive the early game.  Get to level 10 with an
artifact and negative armor class and experience level around
10.  Hang around an alter until hell freezes over.  Possibly
lug up the odd bag of tricks or wand of create monster.  Get
whatever artifact your alter offers.  Dance with a troll or
two.  Dip a longsword.  Kill a batch or two of orcs, and
perhaps a barracks.  Become invisible.  Get a nice ring or
two.  Get either a WoP or a wish (with which you get a WoP).
Spend all your spare time, and some not-so-spare time for the
next week polypiling.

After you get all intrinsics, all abilities maxed, all spells,
armor class -35 or better, +5 or better Excalibur, Stormbringer,
and Mjollnir,  lots of genocide scrolls, experience class 25 or
better, going to hell is like a migraine sufferer having a
hangover.  It's barely noticable.

There really needs to be some balance between the near certainty
of sudden unavoidable death (making it a game of luck) and the
present near certainty of being able to win, given near-infinite
time.  I mean, if I spent only reasonable amounts of time on
the game, I would be unable to win between releases.

-- Don Varvel (varvel@cs.utexas.edu)


